      "*   **Utilitarianism:**  Focuses on maximizing overall happiness or well-being for the greatest number of people.  Which action produces the best consequences for the most stakeholders?  Requires carefully weighing benefits and harms.\n",
      "*   **Deontology (Duty-Based Ethics):**  Emphasizes moral duties and principles, regardless of consequences.  Are there universal moral rules that apply, such as \"Do not lie,\" \"Respect autonomy,\" or \"Do not harm\"? Which action aligns best with these duties, even if the consequences aren't ideal?\n",
      "*   **Virtue Ethics:**  Focuses on the character of the moral agent.  What action would a virtuous person take in this situation?  This requires reflecting on the virtues relevant to the situation, such as compassion, honesty, courage, and justice.\n",
      "*   **Rights-Based Ethics:**  Emphasizes the inherent rights of individuals.  Which action best protects and respects the rights of all stakeholders, especially vulnerable groups?  This often requires balancing competing rights.\n",
